  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using Landsat imagery from 2004 to 2024, this study measured land use changes and surface temperatures in Cuttack, India, finding that built-up areas expanded by 50.52% from 48.95 km² to 73.68 km². Over this two-decade period, the mean land surface temperature increased from 30.89 °C to 42 °C, with 40% of the urban area exhibiting strong ecological thermal stress based on the Urban Thermal Field Variance Index. Normalised difference built-up index values correlated positively with land surface temperature, whereas water indices showed a negative correlation.",
      "why": "It quantifies a 11.11 °C surge in surface temperature for a rapidly urbanising secondary Indian city, replacing vague assumptions of warming with precise long-term thermal index measurements."
    }
  },
